<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Authors>Andrew J. Willshire</Authors>
    <Company>Diametrical Ltd</Company>
    <Copyright>Copyright 2026 Diametrical Ltd</Copyright>
    <!-- 0.5.0 (Phase 330 — the interaction-correlation spine): BREAKING on two
         public surfaces, pre-1.0 so it rides a minor bump.
         `IFuaranTelemetrySink` gains a sixth abstract member
         (`RecordValidateOutcome`), per the pre-1.0-minor-add precedent this
         interface documents for itself — every direct implementer adds
         `member _.RecordValidateOutcome _ = ()`. And
         `Render.RenderContext` gains a required `SessionContext` field, so a
         host constructing the record literally stops compiling until it adds
         `SessionContext = Map.empty`. Consumers repin. -->
    <!-- 0.6.0 (Phase 330, second slice): additive —
         `Render.renderWithSourcesSinkAndContext`, the correlation-aware render
         entry a host calls to thread its opaque SessionContext. Additive, but a
         public-contract addition never rides a same-version repack (the
         workspace producer-side pinning rule), and 0.5.0 was already published.
         -->
    <!-- 0.7.0 (Phase 725): `FormFieldKind` gains a `DateRange` case — the
         single-control date range. Additive on the wire (no existing emission
         changes meaning), but a new DU case breaks every consumer's exhaustive
         `match` over `FormFieldKind`, which is a source break under F#'s
         `TreatWarningsAsErrors` even though STABILITY.md classifies DU
         exhaustiveness as non-breaking. Either way it is a public-contract
         addition, so it takes its own version rather than repacking 0.6.0. -->
    <!-- 0.8.0 (Phases 692-694 — the swap onto the IDL-generated types): BREAKING
         across the whole `Fuaran.UI` type surface, pre-1.0 so it rides a minor
         bump. `Types.fs` no longer DEFINES the wire vocabulary; it ABBREVIATES
         `Fuaran.UI.Generated` (the IDL emission). One version covers the whole
         swap, per the staged plan — not one per stage. See STABILITY.md
         "Recorded change - 0.8.0". Consumers repin and expect source edits at
         record literals; the smart-ctor authoring surface is unchanged (the
         `samples/demo` application has ZERO source changes across the swap). -->
    <!-- 0.9.0 — ADDITIVE renderer surface (no consumer edits): the scope-aware
         render entry that also carries a telemetry sink
         (`renderWithSourcesInScopeAndSink`), and the host-pluggable `GuestSeam`
         that puts a rendered `Mount` guest behind the host's capability policy.
         See STABILITY.md "Recorded change - 0.9.0". -->
    <!-- 0.10.0 — the op-stream slice: DAG checkpoint verification + the
         `OpStream.Abstractions` decode leg. MINOR, and the axis is worth
         stating because two of the four changes look major at a glance.
         (1) `DagReplayError` gains `SnapshotHashMismatch` — a new DU case, so a
         source break under `TreatWarningsAsErrors`, which STABILITY.md's Semver
         section classifies as NON-breaking and 0.7.0 already settled on exactly
         this shape. (2) `GuestExport.FormatVersion` 1 -> 2 refuses v1 documents
         by name, but `Fuaran.UI.OpStream.Replay` is an explicitly UNSTABLE
         surface, so it carries no semver promise and cannot drive the axis up.
         (3) `replayFromCheckpoint` now VERIFIES `SnapshotHash` where it
         previously never read it — a behavioural tightening of the 0.3.3 class.
         (4) Additive `OpStream.Abstractions` surface (`StreamEntry.decode`,
         `coreWitnessWith`) — the feature addition that makes this a minor rather
         than the patch a tightening alone would take. The canonical wire did NOT
         move: `chainFormatVersion` stays 2, the `DagOpRecord` pre-image is
         untouched, no fixture changed. See STABILITY.md "Recorded change -
         0.10.0". -->
    <!-- 0.11.0 — ADDITIVE wire vocabulary: `CellKindErased.TonedPill`, the
         declarative twin of the closure-only `Pill` (a `field` naming the row
         property + a value→tone `map` + an omit-when-default `default`). MINOR
         on exactly the 0.7.0 axis: a new case in a matched DU is an `FS0025`
         source break under `TreatWarningsAsErrors`, which STABILITY.md's Semver
         section classifies as non-breaking. No existing emission changes bytes —
         a tree with no `TonedPill` cell encodes exactly as at 0.10.0. The typed
         author facade `CellKind` gains the twin case and `Column.withTonedPill`;
         the C# `Column` facade gains `WithTonedPill`. See STABILITY.md
         "Recorded change - 0.11.0". -->
    <!-- 0.12.0 — BREAKING (fuaran#665, typed row-source encoding): grid/chart rows
         leave the residual-"<opaque>" boundary. `GridSpec.Source` / `ChartSpec.Source`
         are `Binding<Row seq>` (`Row = Fuaran.Core.Row = Map<string, obj>`), the
         row-consuming closure slots (`ColumnErased.Value`, `RowKey`, the
         `CellKindErased` arms) retype `obj` → `Row`, `Fuaran.grid` takes a REQUIRED
         `toRow: 'row -> Row` projection, and the typed facade's `Column`/`CellKind`
         drop their `'row` parameter (accessors read the projected `Row` by name).
         Wire-ADDITIVE with read-compat: emissions gain structured rows where they
         carried the sentinel; decoders accept `"<opaque>"` indefinitely (→ the
         empty feed). See STABILITY.md "Recorded change - 0.12.0".

         0.13.0 (fuaran#781) - wire resource limits. New module
         `Fuaran.UI.WireLimits` (the F# expression of WIRE_FORMAT.md section 21);
         `DecodeErrorCode` gains `LIMIT_EXCEEDED` and `PreEmitDefect` gains
         `MaxDepthExceeded` (FUARAN091) - both additive DU cases, so MINOR per
         the Semver note in STABILITY.md. It also NARROWS what the decoder
         accepts (over-deep / oversized payloads are now refused rather than
         decoded), which is why it advances the version rather than repacking
         0.12.0. See STABILITY.md "Recorded change - 0.13.0".

         0.14.0 (fuaran#782) - BREAKING: the dispatch gate fails closed.
         `IFuaranRuntime.CanDispatch` returned `true` in every shipped runtime,
         which is the inverse of the published default-deny posture; it now
         DENIES in DiagnosticRuntime / MutableRuntime / BrowserRuntime /
         DriverServices.create / BoundedServices.create, and the permissive
         posture is reached by NAME (Runtime.permissive, MutableRuntime.Permissive,
         BrowserRuntime.createPermissive, *Services.createPermissive) so
         re-enabling it is greppable. `ActionDescriptor` also gains four cases
         (Notify / SetState / WriteToClipboard / CommitLocal), closing the set of
         wire-survivable actions that reach the gate; `Action.Navigate` is
         sanitised on the action path; and a host-reserved State-key namespace
         (`StateKeys.HostReservedPrefix`) is unaddressable from a tree write.
         The break is BEHAVIOURAL, not signature-level - a host compiles and then
         refuses the actions it used to perform, which is the loudest safe way for
         this change to arrive. See STABILITY.md "Recorded breaking change -
         0.14.0" + docs/migrations/782-default-deny-dispatch-gate.md.

         0.15.0 (fuaran#783) - BREAKING: Mount + custom-renderer isolation, the
         two surfaces that sit outside the dispatch gate structurally so 782's
         inversion did not reach them. A decoded Mount is CLAMPED to OutOnly
         (ChannelDirection is a required wire field, so a hostile tree simply
         wrote TwoWay; TwoWay is now a host grant via the new
         GuestSeam.GrantTwoWay, and the downgrade is recorded). With no GuestSeam
         installed a guest now receives Runtime.UnprivilegedGuestRuntime rather
         than the HOST's runtime unwrapped. Both custom-renderer registries key
         on (scope, moduleId, componentId) with NO cross-scope fallback, and
         IFuaranRuntime gains TryRenderCustomInScope /
         TryGetCustomRendererInScope (fail-closed for a hand-written implementer
         that has not added them - deliberate). ServerRenderContext gains a
         required Scope field. And the hash bypass is closed: strictness is a
         host FLOOR (Fuaran.UI.Renderer.CustomHash) a tree may only tighten, and
         under an enforcing floor an unverifiable hash is a refusal - the floor
         still defaults to AdvisoryWarning, so enforcement is opt-in.
         See STABILITY.md "Recorded breaking change - 0.15.0" +
         docs/migrations/783-mount-custom-renderer-isolation.md.
         0.15.1 - additive: INVALID_JSON arithmetic cause-sniffing (the
         didactic names inline arithmetic like `"value": 178 / 180` instead
         of a bare offset - the one repair-envelope class with no cause).
         0.16.0 - additive behaviour (cat:Fuaran.UI.ProgressFieldCell): a
         decoded Progress grid cell in a field-carrying column derives its
         per-row fraction from the row field (clamp 0..1) instead of the
         silent zero fill; no wire-shape change.
         0.17.0 (fuaran#800) - additive package CONTENT: the Fuaran.UI.Renderer
         package gains `content/fuaran-reference-tables.js`, the reference
         table-sort enhancement over the server-rendered `.fuaran-table` markup
         (a dependency-free, ES5, no-build-step progressive enhancement a host
         serves as a file), plus the `[data-sortable]` / `[aria-sort]` indicator
         affordances in `content/fuaran-reference.css`. No managed API, wire
         shape or emission changes, and no existing page changes appearance: the
         new CSS keys ONLY off attributes the script sets, so it cannot match
         until the script is served. It rides a minor rather than a patch
         because a new packaged asset is a public-contract ADDITION - a
         consumer can now link something the previous version could not
         supply - and an addition never rides a same-version repack. 
         0.18.1 - additive: INVALID_JSON envelope de-dup (the innermost
         "parse error at offset N" propagates unwrapped - one occurrence per
         envelope; didactic tails unchanged).
         0.19.0 (fuaran#812) - protected email links (scraper-resistant
         mailto emission).
         0.20.0 (fuaran#815) - additive decode leniency: the Transform
         `source` slot accepts a State/Static/Bound WRAPPER (unwrapped to
         its defaultValue/value - initial-snapshot semantics) and ROW-MAJOR
         data (an array of row objects, transposed to canonical columnar) -
         the two shapes three model families emitted organically in the
         Tier-D pilot (2026-08-13). Host-bridge normalisation only; Core,
         the wire spec and canonical emissions are untouched. Its own
         version rather than joining the unreleased 0.19.0, per the
         same-version-repack rule.
         0.21.0 (fuaran#819 + fuaran#821) - additive wire vocabulary, one
         version for the change-set: `Duration` in BOTH format vocabularies
         (`Format` + `CellFormat`, unit Seconds/Minutes/Hours x style
         Compact/Clock/Long, locale-independent shared rendering) plus
         `CellFormat.RelativeTime` cell-vocabulary parity (819); and the
         standalone `Icon` display kind (`IconSpec`: icon + IconSize +
         tone + optional a11y label, decorative-by-default) (821). New DU
         cases break consumers' exhaustive matches under
         TreatWarningsAsErrors, so it takes its own version per the
         additive-DU-case precedent (0.7.0/0.11.0).
         0.22.0 (fuaran#820) - behavioural addition, submit-payload
         semantics: a `Call` / `Notify` in a Form's `onSubmit` receives the
         form's field values keyed by field id (Notify: merged into its
         payload under a "fields" key, authored key wins; Call: the body
         `{"fields": {...}}` through the server-driven driver's new
         `DriverServices.InterpretSubmitCall` seam - `None` default falls
         back to `InterpretHostEffect`, the pre-820 behaviour). No wire
         change. `DriverServices` gains a record field (hosts constructing
         via the documented `create ... with` pattern are unaffected), and
         a public-contract addition never rides a same-version repack
         (the 0.6.0 precedent), so it takes its own version.
         0.23.0 (fuaran#818) - the reactive-derivation first cut, one
         rule (operator-approved 2026-08-13: O1+O2+O3 together, the
         `valueFrom` field name, and the grid-sort header affordance
         pulled INTO the first cut): any read slot that takes a literal
         may take a Binding, evaluated with subscription semantics, the
         Transform verbs the only computation vocabulary. O1 - Transform
         sources shaped as State/Selection/Query bindings are PRESERVED
         (`TransformSource.Data | Live`) and re-evaluated live (the 815
         snapshot upgraded in place; canonical re-encode reproduces the
         binding-shaped source byte-for-byte). O3 - `SetState.valueFrom`
         (value XOR valueFrom; dispatch-time evaluation inside the
         existing gate). Plus `DataGridSpec.sortStateKey` (sortable
         data-bound grid headers writing a `{column, direction}` state
         descriptor; runtime-side row sort). O2 (`Switch.on`) had
         already shipped as fuaran#768. Source-breaking DU/record
         changes (`Binding.Transform` source slot, `Action.SetState`
         arity, `DataGridSpec` field) are pre-1.0 minor per the 0.5.0
         precedent; the wire is additive. 
         0.23.1 - additive leniency: an EMPTY array default on a live
         Transform source (defaultValue: []) decodes to the empty table,
         as Query/Selection sources do - an initially-empty live collection
         is a complete intent with zero rows and no columns to infer
         (observed organically: the Tier-D cohort r0 count badge, terra).
         0.24.0 - fuaran#850 implied-node-close decode recovery: on an
         INVALID_JSON node payload matching the dropped-node-wrapper-brace
         class (children[]/cases[]/root boundary), ancestor-legal-token
         auto-close repairs the owed wrappers, counted under the new
         JsonDecode.Reliance surface (id "implied-node-close"); bounded,
         profile-gated, fails closed to the original error. Additive
         decode behaviour + additive public surface (pre-1.0 minor, the
         0.20.0 leniency precedent).
         0.25.0 - the bounded program path LEAVES Fuaran.UI.ServerDriven:
         `BoundedActions` (the bounded-Action interpreter), `resolveTree`
         (binding re-resolution) and `BoundedDriver` (the no-'Msg loop)
         move whole to the program domain's Fuaran.Program.Bounded, so a
         single interpreter serves both placements of that loop instead of
         one per host. A REMOVAL from this package's public surface -
         consumers of the bounded path reference Fuaran.Program.Bounded
         instead; the transport core (DomPatch / ClientEffect / Lowering /
         Validation / Driver / Channel) is untouched. The dependency runs
         one way (program -> UI, never back), which is what the split buys:
         see that repo's DECISIONS.md D5. Pre-1.0 minor.

         0.26.0 - fuaran#855 uniqueness-gated over-close recovery: 850's
         mirror with the OPPOSITE default. On an INVALID_JSON node payload
         that is net over-closed by one or two closers, the candidate
         single/multi-deletion repairs are enumerated within stated bounds,
         de-duplicated by parsed value and decoded; the repair is accepted
         IFF EXACTLY ONE decodes clean, and refused - original error
         unchanged - otherwise. Counted both ways under distinct
         JsonDecode.Reliance ids ("over-close-unique", "over-close-refused").
         Additive decode behaviour + additive public surface (pre-1.0
         minor, the same precedent).

         0.29.0 - fuaran#889 the user-action record: `ActionInvocation` +
         `IActionInvocationSink` (a NEW interface, so no existing implementer
         is touched) + the durable `ActionInvocationSqliteSink`. BREAKING on
         two record surfaces, the 0.5.0 shape exactly: `Render.RenderContext`
         gains required `ActionSink` + `CurrentNodeId` fields, and
         `DriverServices` gains a required `ActionRecording` field - a host
         constructing either record literally stops compiling until it names
         them (`None` everywhere reproduces 0.28.0 behaviour exactly). Also
         behavioural: `Validation.describeAction` delegates to
         `ActionInvocation.describe`, so a dispatch-denied reject reason now
         prints `Navigate`'s PATH rather than its whole route. Pre-1.0 so it
         rides a minor bump; consumers repin. See STABILITY.md.

         (Authored against 0.27.0 as 0.28.0; renumbered on rebase because a
         concurrent session had already released 0.28.0 for the accessible
         chart root summary. Two sessions minting the same version is the
         same class of collision as two minting the same phase number - the
         fix is to renumber, never to share the slot.)

         0.30.0 - dependency floor: the Fuaran.Core.* substrate pins move
         0.3.0 -> 0.6.0, so every published Fuaran.UI.* package ships a
         higher Core dependency floor. No managed API, wire shape or
         emission changes - but the DELIVERED CONTENT moves, and delivered
         content never rides a same-version repack.

         0.31.0 - dependency floor, same axis as 0.30.0: the Fuaran.Core.*
         substrate pins move 0.6.0 -> 0.11.0. The intervening Core releases
         are additive on every surface this tier consumes (a read-side
         opt-in on the Wire strand, the columnar delta vocabulary and its
         incremental evaluator, an added conformance family), so the whole
         solution compiles against them unchanged and no managed API, wire
         shape or emission moves here. The DELIVERED CONTENT does, and
         delivered content never rides a same-version repack.

         0.32.0 - additive public surface in Fuaran.UI.Renderer: the
         declared-affordance provider seam (Fuaran.UI.Renderer.Affordances - a
         registration point + vocabulary that holds no declarations of its own),
         `window.__fuaran.getAffordances(moduleId?)` on the debug surface
         (surface version 0.1.0 -> 0.2.0), and the relay's `read.affordances`
         entry point, which advances the RELAY PROFILE to relay@1.1. Nothing is
         removed or retyped. The one existing-shape change is a field added to
         `Relay.RelaySurface`, which is DEBUG-only / unstable and excluded from
         semver (STABILITY.md); `Relay.surfaceOf` fills it, so a host that
         builds its surface the supported way is unaffected. All 24 shipped
         relay@1.0 corpus fixtures pass unchanged against the 1.1 peer - the
         negotiation change (a peer now answers `hello` at the highest profile
         the CLIENT accepts, rather than only at its own) is what keeps that
         true.

         0.35.0 - fuaran#1032 markdown egress coverage. BREAKING RENDERING
         BEHAVIOUR under the default policy, and additive on the public
         surface. `Markdown.toHtmlWithEgress` is new; the pure
         `Markdown.toHtml` survives UNCHANGED as the permissive case
         (`toHtmlWithEgress permissiveEgress`, byte-for-byte across the whole
         cross-host corpus). What moves is the three RENDERER call sites -
         client, SSR, email - which now pass their context's `EgressPolicy`,
         whose default denies. So a decoded markdown body whose link or image
         names an undeclared origin renders
         `about:blank#fuaran-egress-refused` plus a
         `data-fuaran-egress-refused` marker where it previously rendered the
         destination. This closes the one gap 0.33.0 disclosed in three
         places. The scheme floor's own answer is deliberately unchanged - a
         URL the floor rejects is still the bare `about:blank`, no marker.

         (Authored against 0.33.0 as 0.34.0; renumbered because 0.34.0 had
         already been cut for the additive validator findings. A breaking
         rendering change folded into a minor whose declared content says
         "additive" would misdescribe the slot, and the slot is what a
         consumer reads.) -->
